// ===========================================================================
//  sounds.json reference (all fields and every valid form)
//  ============================= IMPORTANT =============================
//  This is a **documentation file**. The program never reads it, which is
//  why comments are allowed here.
//  The real config file, sounds.json, must be strict JSON — it does NOT
//  support comments, so copying this file straight over your config would
//  make the bridge exit on startup with an invalid-JSON error.
//
//  How to use it: read the forms below, then hand-write your own
//  ~/.config/kdeconnect-popup-bridge/sounds.json;
//  if you'd rather copy something verbatim, use the clean JSON in the
//  "Sound rules" section of README.md.
//  Chinese readers see sounds.json.example.
// ===========================================================================


// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 0. Where the file goes
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   Default path: ~/.config/kdeconnect-popup-bridge/sounds.json
//   Custom path:  set the env var KC_BRIDGE_CONFIG=/some/path.json
//   A missing file is fine — the bridge still runs (everything muted, no app
//   disabled) and does not complain about a first run.


// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. Minimal form: sound only (string form)
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   Each key in apps is an app name; the value is the absolute path to a
//   sound file. default is the fallback used when no app-specific rule matches.
//
//   {
//     "apps": {
//       "WeChat": "/home/you/Documents/sfx/mine.ogg"
//     },
//     "default": ""
//   }
//
//   "default": ""                  ← empty string = mute every other app
//                                    (the most common pick)
//   "default": "/x/default.ogg"    ← every other app uses this sound


// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. Full form: object form (per-app tuning)
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   Use an object when you need to change how long the popup stays, its
//   urgency, or to silence an app entirely.
//
//   {
//     "apps": {
//       "WeChat":     { "sound": "/x/a.ogg",     "timeout": 30, "urgency": "normal",   "enabled": true },
//       "StockApp":   { "sound": "/x/stock.ogg", "timeout": 20 },
//       "Telegram":   { "sound": "",             "urgency": "critical" },
//       "SpammyChat": { "enabled": false }
//     },
//     "default": { "sound": "", "timeout": 10 }
//   }


// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. Both forms can be mixed
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   A string value sets only the sound; an object value is a full rule. Both
//   may live side by side in the same file:
//
//   {
//     "apps": {
//       "WeChat":   "/x/a.ogg",                            // string: sound only, rest from default
//       "StockApp": { "sound": "/x/b.ogg", "timeout": 20 } // object: override only what you want
//     },
//     "default": { "sound": "", "timeout": 10 }
//   }
//
//   Here WeChat plays /x/a.ogg but inherits default's timeout of 10s, while
//   StockApp sets 20s itself and stops inheriting. The final rule is always
//   "default as the base, app entries override field by field".


// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 4. Field reference
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   sound    Absolute path to the sound file.
//            Empty string "" or omitted = this app is muted (popup only, no audio).
//
//   timeout  How long the popup stays, in seconds; decimals allowed (e.g. 2.5).
//            Converted to milliseconds for notify-send --expire-time (or
//            expire_timeout on the native D-Bus backend).
//            Omitted falls back to the --timeout CLI flag, then to the
//            built-in default of 10 seconds.
//
//   urgency  low / normal / critical. Defaults to normal.
//            ⚠️ critical notifications never auto-dismiss in KDE, which makes
//               timeout pointless — don't set critical if you want a timed popup.
//
//   enabled  false = ignore this app entirely (no popup, no sound). Defaults to true.
//            Omitting it is fine too; { "enabled": false } exists purely to block an app.
//
//   Priority: app-specific rule  >  default  >  --timeout CLI flag  >  built-in 10s


// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 5. What to put in the app name
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   Use the appName reported by KDE Connect — not the package name, and not
//   the localized display name. Unsure what it actually is? Print it:
//
//       kdeconnect-popup-bridge --dump      # list current notifications and their apps
//       kdeconnect-popup-bridge --verbose   # print app=... for each notification at runtime
//
//   Matching: an exact match is tried first, then a case-insensitive match
//   (writing WeChat catches wechat). Non-ASCII app names are fine — JSON is
//   UTF-8, so write them as-is.


// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 6. Gotchas
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   ● Restart for changes to take effect
//     The config is read once at process start; editing it mid-run does nothing:
//         systemctl --user restart 'app-kdeconnect\x2dpopup\x2dbridge@autostart.service'
//
//   ● Never set timeout to 0
//     0 has muddled meaning in the freedesktop spec (notify-send treats it as
//     "close now", other implementations as "never expire").
//     To get the system default back, just delete the timeout field.
//
//   ● No comments in this file
//     The parser is strict JSON — any // or /* */ breaks the whole config and
//     the bridge aborts with "config error". The symptom is that no
//     notifications come through at all after boot.
//
//   ● Keep notes in an extra key instead of a comment
//     load() only reads apps and default; other top-level keys are ignored
//     without complaint, e.g.
//         { "_memo": "WeChat uses the mining sound", "apps": {...}, "default": "" }
//     ⚠️ But once you edit the config with --set-sound, the program rebuilds the
//        whole file and such keys are dropped. Likewise, sound-only rules get
//        rewritten in string form and lose their object shape.
//
//   ● --list-sounds shows sounds only, not timeout / urgency
//     Verify those two by sending a real notification rather than trusting this command.
//
//   ● You can write rules from the CLI (no manual file editing)
//         --set-sound WeChat=/x/a.ogg     write
//         --set-sound WeChat=             delete this app's rule
//         --set-sound default=            mute every other app
